@page "/docs/components/toolbar"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.Toolbar
<PageTitle>Toolbar - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Toolbar</h1>
            <DocBadges Slug="toolbar" />
        </div>
        <p>Groups related controls behind a single tab stop. Tab moves focus into the bar once, and the arrow keys move between every button, toggle, link and input inside it. A Toggle Group placed in the bar hands its keyboard handling over automatically, so the two never compete for the arrow keys.</p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(ToolbarUsageExample)"
                Description="Build the bar from toggle groups, buttons, links and separators. Give it an `aria-label` as well: once a page has more than one toolbar, screen reader users rely on the name to tell them apart." />

    <CompositionTree Root="Anatomy"
                     Description="The bar accepts any mix of controls. Separators orient themselves across the bar, and `BzToolbarGroup` lets related controls share a disabled state and an `aria-label`." />

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Handling overflow</h2>
        <p>When a toolbar runs out of width, <code>Overflow</code> decides what happens to the controls that no longer fit. There are three modes:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Wrap</strong> (the default) moves them onto additional rows.</li>
            <li><strong>Scroll</strong> keeps a single row and adds chevron buttons to page through it. The chevrons can sit on either edge or together at the end, and each one dims once there is nothing left to scroll in its direction.</li>
            <li><strong>Expand</strong> shows one row and hides the rest behind a toggle at the end. The expanded state supports two-way binding, so code outside the bar can open it too.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Controls that appear while the page is in use, such as a contextual action, should not end up hidden behind the toggle. <code>Reveal</code> handles this. With <code>Pin</code>, the new control moves onto the visible row. With <code>Expand</code>, the bar opens while the control sits on a hidden row and collapses again once it is gone. Opening a menu from the visible row does not expand the bar.</p>
        <p>The chevrons and the toggle only appear while the controls actually overflow, so they disappear as soon as the bar fits. Keyboard users never need them: moving focus with the arrow keys scrolls the focused control into view, and in Expand mode the bar stays open while focus is on a hidden row.</p>
    </section>

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Default"
          Description="A formatting bar with single and multiple selection toggle groups, action buttons, a link and separators. Focus any control and use the arrow keys to move through the whole bar. Home and End jump to the first and last control, and because `Loop` is on by default, the arrows wrap around at either end."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarDefaultExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Vertical"
          Description="Set `Orientation` to `Vertical` to stack the controls. The Up and Down arrows take over navigation, and the separators turn horizontal on their own."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarVerticalExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Disabled"
          Description="You can disable a single control, a group or the entire bar. By default a disabled control cannot be hovered or focused, and the arrow keys skip it. Set `FocusableWhenDisabled` on a control, as Redo does here, to keep it reachable and announced as unavailable, which helps users understand why it is off."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarDisabledExample)" />

    <Demo Title="With an input"
          Description="Inside an input, the Left and Right arrows move the caret as usual. Once the caret reaches the start or end of the text, the next press moves focus to the neighboring control, so the field never traps the keyboard. Home and End always stay with the caret."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarInputExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Overflow"
          Description="Switch between the three overflow modes and resize the window to see each one. The button above the bar opens it through the bound expanded state, and the contextual buttons show `Reveal` in action."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarOverflowExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Contextual"
          Description="A toolbar that floats above the content it acts on, built from a `HoverCard` with a frameless bar inside. It opens above the phrase and flips below when there is not enough room. Hover over the phrase or focus it with the keyboard to try it. For a bar fixed to the screen instead, use `Position`: `BottomCenter`, for example, gives you a dock that slides in from the bottom edge."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarFloatingExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Mixed and custom items"
          Description="Any focusable component can join the arrow key order. Add `data-bz-roving-item` to it, and every Blaizio component passes the attribute on to its focusable element. Here a split button, a dropdown menu and a select sit next to a regular toolbar button, while the badge has no marker and is skipped."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarMixedExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Customization"
          Description="Every part accepts `Class`. This media player uses it for a pill-shaped bar, round buttons, a filled play button and a taller separator. The same approach works for floating toolbars."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarCustomizationExample)" />

    <Demo Title="RTL"
          Description="Inside a right-to-left `DirectionProvider`, the bar starts from the right and the Left and Right arrows swap meaning to match. Switching direction takes effect immediately, without re-creating the toolbar."
          Example="typeof(ToolbarRtlExample)" />
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly CompositionNode Anatomy = new("BzToolbar",
        new CompositionNode("BzToggleGroup",
            new CompositionNode("BzToggleGroupItem")),
        new CompositionNode("BzToolbarSeparator"),
        new CompositionNode("BzToolbarGroup",
            new CompositionNode("BzToolbarButton")),
        new CompositionNode("BzToolbarLink"),
        new CompositionNode("BzToolbarInput"));
}
